    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. one more thing--in some cases, the landlord might argue that the lockdown doesn't count as force majeure because the \"event\" (the virus) was technically foreseeable once the initial outbreak was reported. how do courts usually weigh that \"foreseeability\" argument when determining if the clause is actually triggered?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Courts generally distinguish between the foreseeability of a *threat* and the unforeseeability of the *specific governmental response* or disruption. While the virus itself might be foreseeable, the extent, duration, and specific regulatory mandates (like a full lockdown) are often deemed beyond the reasonable control of the parties when they signed the lease. The focus tends to be on whether the specific governmental interference makes continued business operation impossible or commercially impracticable, rather than merely on the initial possibility of the virus.",
      "source": "target",
