    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. what about the \"unforeseeable\" aspect of \"acts of god\"? i've seen some old legal texts use pretty vague language there. does that vagueness ever allow a party to claim that more \"chaotic\" or unpredictable environmental disruptions—things that aren't strictly natural disasters but are still totally out of human control—could fall under that umbrella?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The term \"Acts of God\" is traditionally confined to truly natural, overwhelming events like hurricanes or earthquakes. Courts are very hesitant to stretch this language to cover economic fallout or societal disruptions caused by human action, even if those actions feel chaotic. If disruptive events are not purely natural, parties should rely on a custom-drafted force majeure clause or a separate event-of-default provision to cover unpredictable, non-natural disruptions. Therefore, relying on the ambiguity of \"Acts of God\" for modern, systemic disruptions is a very risky legal gamble.",
      "source": "target",
